[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Collections Framework & Concurrent Collections in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effective data management is crucial for high-performance Android applications. The Java Collections Framework provides standard data structures like List, Set, and Map. However, in modern Android development involving multi-threading, background tasks (RxJava, Kotlin Coroutines, or Java threads), standard collections can throw ConcurrentModificationExceptions or lead to inconsistent states. This tutorial explores standard collections and thread-safe concurrent alternatives."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Standard Collections Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Standard collections such as ArrayList, HashMap, and HashSet are designed for single-threaded or properly synchronized environments. They offer high performance and low overhead because they do not incur the synchronization penalty."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"List<String> userList = new ArrayList<>();
userList.add("Alice");
userList.add("Bob");

for (String user : userList) {
    Log.d("Collection", user);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Danger of Concurrent Modification"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"If multiple threads access a standard collection concurrently, and at least one thread modifies it structurally, it must be synchronized externally. Failing to do so results in unpredictable behavior or a ConcurrentModificationException."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// DANGEROUS IN MULTI-THREADED ENVIRONMENTS
List<String> unsafeList = new ArrayList<>();

new Thread(() -> {
    unsafeList.add("Item 1");
}).start();

new Thread(() -> {
    for (String item : unsafeList) {
        // May throw ConcurrentModificationException
        Log.d("Unsafe", item);
    }
}).start();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Concurrent Collections"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To safely handle shared data across multiple threads without explicit external synchronization blocks, Java provides the java.util.concurrent package. These classes use sophisticated locking mechanisms (like lock striping or compare-and-swap operations) to maximize concurrency."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ConcurrentHashMap: A thread-safe replacement for HashMap that allows concurrent reads and updates without locking the entire map."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"CopyOnWriteArrayList: A thread-safe variant of ArrayList in which all mutative operations (add, set, etc.) are implemented by making a fresh copy of the underlying array. Ideal for scenarios where traversals vastly outnumber writes (e.g., observer lists)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BlockingQueue / ConcurrentLinkedQueue: Thread-safe queues ideal for producer-consumer patterns commonly found in background processing."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Practical Implementation: ConcurrentHashMap"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how to use ConcurrentHashMap in an Android ViewModel or background worker to safely store and update shared state."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class AnalyticsManager {
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, Integer> eventCounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void trackEvent(String eventName) {
        eventCounts.compute(eventName, (key, value) -> (value == null) ? 1 : value + 1);
    }

    public int getEventCount(String eventName) {
        return eventCounts.getOrDefault(eventName, 0);
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Practical Implementation: CopyOnWriteArrayList"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use CopyOnWriteArrayList when managing lists of listeners or callbacks where modifications are rare, but iteration happens frequently on various threads."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;

public class EventBus {
    private final List<EventListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();

    public void register(EventListener listener) {
        listeners.add(listener);
    }

    public void unregister(EventListener listener) {
        listeners.remove(listener);
    }

    public void publishEvent(String data) {
        for (EventListener listener : listeners) {
            listener.onEvent(data);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"6. Best Practices for Android"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid premature synchronization: Do not use concurrent collections on the Main (UI) thread if data is only accessed locally within that thread. Stick to ArrayList or HashMap for better performance."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Memory considerations: Remember that CopyOnWriteArrayList creates a new array copy on every write operation. Frequent writes will cause excessive garbage collection."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Android-optimized structures where applicable: For mapping primitives to objects, consider Android's SparseArray instead of HashMap<Integer, Object> to avoid autoboxing overhead in single-threaded contexts."}]
